> Following up on this thread to confirm that compRelSEM() no longer provides the reliability estimate for second-order factors, along with reliability estimates for the first-order factors, by including the higher = argument. Rather, to get a reliability estimate for the second-order factor, you need to use the W = argument with the custom weight syntax (https://www.rdocumentation.org/packages/semTools/versions/0.5-9/topics/compRelSEM).

Confirmed.

> Also, when the compRelSEM() function is used on a second-order CFA, are the values returned for each first-order factor interpreted as the omega reliability estimates for that first-order factor accounting for the second-order factor?

I don't know what you mean by "accounting for".  If you mean "controlling for", I don't think that conforms to the higher-order model.

By default, any detected higher-order factor(s) would be assumed the source of true-score variance.  The default output format is verbose, indicating what source(s) of variance constitutes the numerator.
>From the help-page example:

mod.hi <- ' visual  =~ x1 + x2 + x3
            textual =~ x4 + x5 + x6
            speed   =~ x7 + x8 + x9
            general =~ visual + textual + speed '
fit.hi <- cfa(mod.hi, data = HolzingerSwineford1939)
compRelSEM(fit.hi)

$visual

