> So far we followed the identification strategy described by Liu et al (2017) (who adapt the framework from Millsap & Tein, 2004 for the longitudinal case)

Nope, don't do that.
For binary items, you cannot distinguish between DIF in thresholds, loadings, and intercepts. So you need to compare a scalar/strong invariance model (that constrains all 3, but releases unnecessary identification constraints on residual variances) directly to any minimally identified configural model.  You can use the semTools::measEq.syntax() function as a pedagogical guidestone.

Terrence D. Jorgensen
Assistant Professor, Methods and Statistics
Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam
http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen

